      <li>5ml / 0.2oz gin</li>
      <li>25ml / 0.8oz Aperol</li>
      <li>25ml / 0.8oz vermouth</li>
      <li>A slice of orange, to garnish</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>Method</h4>
    <ol>
      <li>Add ice, sloe gin, gin, Aperol and vermouth to a coupe glass.</li>
      <li>Garnish with a slice of orange.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sloe gin is a British red liqueur, made with gin and ripe sloes.</li>
    <li>Sloes (<em>Prunus spinosa</em>) are small fruits related to the plum.</li>
    <li>Sloes are rarely eaten, because they’re tart and astringent.</li>
    <li>Gin is gin, but sloe gin is a gin liqueur.</li>
    <li>Most good sloe gins are sweet, while keeping the gin’s botanicals and juniper flavours.</li>
    <li>The best sloe gin for your money is Gordon’s.</li>
    <li>Sloe gin can be used in cocktails or sipped on its own.</li>
  </ul>

